\f0\fs24 \cf2 The eight wall lamps illuminating the grand salon are true works of art in patinated bronze. Created in 1884, they were originally gas-powered and later adapted for electricity, yet their elegance has remained untouched. Each applique was designed with obsessive attention to detail, as evidenced by the drawings preserved in the museum. The lamps are inspired by Renaissance flag holders in wrought iron, often featuring dragon heads, which the Bagatti Valsecchi brothers had encountered during their travels through central Italian cities. A perfect example of how the brothers blended aesthetics and functionality, merging past and present in a truly innovative way.\
